        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 17: Infrastructure and Automation with Ansible\nP\u00e1ginas: 654-731\n\u2022 Technical requirements  (654)\n\u2022 Introducing Ansible architecture and configuration management  (655)\n  \u2022 Understanding the Ansible architecture  (655)\n  \u2022 Introducing configuration management  (658)\n\u2022 Installing Ansible  (659)\n  \u2022 Installing Ansible on Ubuntu  (660)\n  \u2022 Installing Ansible using pip  (661)\n\u2022 Working with Ansible  (662)\n  \u2022 Setting up the lab environment  (662)\n  \u2022 Configuring Ansible  (665)\n  \u2022 Using Ansible ad hoc commands  (670)\n  \u2022 Exploring Ansible modules  (681)\n  \u2022 Using Ansible playbooks  (683)\n  \u2022 Using templates with Jinja2  (715)\n  \u2022 Creating Ansible roles  (723)\n\u2022 Summary  (729)\n\u2022 Questions  (730)\n\u2022 Further reading  (730)\n17\nInfrastructure and Automation\nwith Ansible\nIf your day-to-day system administration or development work involves tedious and repetitive\noperations, Ansible could help you run your tasks while saving you precious time. Ansible is a tool\nfor automating software provisioning , configuration management , and application deployment\nworkflows . Initially developed by Michael DeHaan in 2012, Ansible was acquired by Red Hat in 2015\nand is now maintained as an open source project.\nIn this chapter, you\u2019ll learn about the fundamental concepts of Ansible, along with a variety of hands-on\nexamples. In particular, we\u2019ll explore the following topics:\n\u2022I Introducing Ansible architecture and configuration management\n\u2022I Installing Ansible\n\u2022I Working with Ansible",
